# Copy to `.audit-private.local` and fill in. That name is gitignored; this
# example is committed and must never contain a real one.
#
# `pnpm m7-check --only=audit` reads it and fails M7-4b if any of these names
# appears in a publishable file - tracked, or untracked and not ignored.
#
# Why the file exists at all. The audit discovers most of what it looks for:
# the account the process runs as, and the repositories sitting beside this
# one. But a discovered repository name is anchored to `repos/<name>`, because
# a bare name would fire on any repository called something ordinary - and that
# anchor is exactly the hole a name mentioned in prose goes through. A comment
# reading "every session in <a private project>" is not a path, and no pattern
# derived from the filesystem can see it. One did reach a public commit that
# way.
#
# So the bare names are written down, and they are written down *here* rather
# than in the driver, because a list of names that must never be published has
# no business in a public repository.
#
# Format: one name per line. Blank lines and `#` comments ignored. Matched as a
# case-insensitive substring with no word boundaries, deliberately - a name
# concatenated into a larger string is still a published name. Anything shorter
# than 4 characters is refused and reported rather than silently used.

# a work project and everything named after it. Pick names that are not
# already fixture names in this repo - `acme` is used as a neutral placeholder
# throughout, so listing it would fire on sixteen innocent files.
northwind
# personal repositories
weekend-game
tiny-tool
